      "text": "We start by multiplying both sides of the equation by $x+2$ to eliminate the fraction: \\begin{align*}\n5(x+2)&=x^3-2x^2-8x\\\\\n5x+10&=x^3-2x^2-8x.\n\\end{align*} We then rearrange the equation as follows: \\[x^3-2x^2-13x-10=0\\] by moving all other terms to one side. We notice that $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomial because substituting $-1$ into the polynomial yields $(-1)^3-2(-1)^2-13(-1)-10=0$. However, this was just a lucky guess, so lets not get too excited yet (as per the principle of moderation).\n\nDividing the polynomial by $x+1$ according to long division yields \\[x^2-3x-10=(x+1)(x-5). \\] Setting $x+1=0$ gives us $x=-1$ as a solution, but setting $x-5=0$ gives us $x=5$ as a solution as well. The sum of the four roots $\\boxed{-7}$. The answer is -7",
